// Original Valus instruction mapped to the 2026–27 HOSA event test plans.
// Practice questions are educational and are not official HOSA questions.
import { courseFrom } from './courses-two.js';

export const COURSES_THREE = {};
const OS = (book, title) => ({title:`OpenStax ${title}`, url:`https://openstax.org/details/books/${book}`});
const course = (name, refs, units) => { COURSES_THREE[name] = courseFrom(name, units, refs); };

course('Health Informatics',[OS('introductory-statistics-2e','Introductory Statistics 2e'),{title:'HHS: HIPAA for professionals',url:'https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/index.html'},{title:'ONC: HealthIT.gov',url:'https://www.healthit.gov/'}],[
 ['Unit 1 · Roles and information systems','History, careers and roles make up 8% of the Health Informatics test plan and information systems in healthcare 12%. Informatics sits between clinical care, data and technology: it asks how information is captured, stored, shared and used to make better decisions.',[
  ['Informatics careers and the shift to electronic records','Health informatics is the use of information, technology and people to improve health and care. Health information management focuses on the accuracy, completeness and lifecycle of records. Clinical informaticists, often nurses or physicians with extra training, help design systems that fit clinical work. Health data analysts turn stored data into reports that answer operational or clinical questions. The move from paper charts to electronic health records was accelerated in the United States by federal incentive programs that rewarded meaningful use of certified systems.','A role is defined by the problem it solves, not just by job title. Someone who maps a lab result code so two systems agree is solving an interoperability problem; someone who checks whether diagnosis codes match documentation is solving a data quality problem. Electronic records offer legibility, remote access and decision support, but they also introduce new risks such as copy-forward errors, alert fatigue and downtime. Good informatics work weighs both sides instead of assuming technology is automatically better.',['A clinic notices that physicians copy the previous visit’s note forward, and outdated medication lists keep reappearing. Which informatics roles should help, and why?','Health information management can audit documentation quality, and a clinical informaticist can redesign the note template and workflow so clinicians review medications each visit. The problem is a mix of data quality and system design, not simply user carelessness.'],[['Health information management','Protects the accuracy, completeness and lifecycle of the record.',['accura','record','audit']],['Clinical informatics','Designs systems and workflows that fit how clinicians actually work.',['workflow','design','template']],['Copy-forward risk','Reusing old text can carry outdated information into the current record.',['copy','outdated','forward']]],['Which role most directly focuses on the accuracy and lifecycle of patient records?',['Health information management professional','Network engineer','Pharmaceutical sales representative','Biomedical equipment installer'],'Health information management specializes in the integrity of records from creation to retention.'],['Which is a known risk introduced by electronic health records?',['Copied-forward outdated information','Illegible handwriting','Inability to search records','Records that exist in only one paper copy'],'Copy-forward functions can spread outdated information; illegibility and single paper copies are paper-record problems.']],
  ['EHRs, interoperability and standards','An electronic health record holds a patient’s clinical information across encounters and can be shared among authorized providers. A personal health record is managed by the patient. Interoperability is the ability of different systems to exchange data and use it meaningfully. Standards make that possible: HL7 and FHIR describe how messages and resources are structured, while code systems such as LOINC for lab tests, SNOMED CT for clinical concepts and ICD-10 for diagnoses give shared meaning to each data element.','Exchanging data is not the same as understanding it. Two hospitals can send each other files successfully, yet if one labels a glucose test with a local code the receiving system may not recognize it as glucose at all. That is the difference between technical exchange and semantic interoperability. Health information exchanges connect organizations in a region; application programming interfaces let approved apps request specific data. Each connection must also respect consent, minimum necessary use and security.',['A patient transfers between hospitals. The receiving team gets the lab results, but the values do not appear in their trend graphs. Explain the likely cause.','The data arrived, so technical exchange worked, but the lab tests were probably coded with local codes rather than a shared standard such as LOINC. The receiving system cannot map them to its own tests, so semantic interoperability failed.'],[['Technical exchange','Systems can successfully send and receive a message or file.',['send','receiv','exchange']],['Semantic interoperability','Both systems interpret the data the same way through shared codes.',['meaning','semantic','interpret']],['Standard code systems','LOINC, SNOMED CT and ICD-10 give each data element a shared meaning.',['loinc','code','standard']]],['What is interoperability?',['The ability of different systems to exchange and use data','A single vendor’s system used everywhere','A backup copy of the database','A paper release-of-information form'],'Interoperability is about exchange plus meaningful use across different systems.'],['Which code system is designed to identify laboratory tests?',['LOINC','CPT for surgery only','A local spreadsheet','RGB'],'LOINC provides standard identifiers for laboratory and clinical observations.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Data analytics','Data analytics carries the most weight in the test plan at 28%. Expect calculations and interpretation from case studies, clinical trials and EHR extracts. Practice with the calculator rules you will have at the event: basic operations and square root only.',[
  ['Describing data: center, spread and displays','Descriptive statistics summarize data. The mean is the arithmetic average, the median is the middle value when ordered, and the mode is the most frequent value. Spread describes variability: the range is maximum minus minimum, and the standard deviation describes typical distance from the mean. Histograms show the shape of numeric data, bar graphs compare categories, and line graphs show change over time.','Choosing the right summary depends on shape. A few very long hospital stays pull the mean upward, so the median is often a better description of a typical stay in skewed data. Always check units and sample size before comparing groups. A chart can mislead if the axis does not start at zero or if bins are uneven. When a question gives raw values, sort them first: it makes the median, range and outliers easy to see.',['Five lengths of stay are 2, 3, 3, 4 and 18 days. Which summary best describes a typical stay, and why?','The mean is 6 days because the 18-day stay pulls it upward. The median is 3 days and better represents a typical stay. The range of 16 days signals an outlier worth investigating.'],[['Median','The middle ordered value resists the pull of extreme values.',['median','middle']],['Skew','A long tail of extreme values moves the mean away from the typical case.',['skew','outlier','extreme']],['Display choice','Histograms show numeric distributions; bar graphs compare categories.',['histogram','bar','graph']]],['For the values 4, 6, 6, 9 and 15, what is the median?',['6','8','9','11'],'Ordered, the middle (third) value is 6. The mean would be 8.'],['Which display best shows the distribution of patient ages?',['Histogram','Pie chart of hospital names','Single number','Organizational chart'],'A histogram groups numeric values into bins to show shape and spread.']],
  ['Rates, risk and measures of association','Health data is often expressed as rates so that groups of different sizes can be compared. Incidence counts new cases in a population over a period, while prevalence counts all existing cases at a point or period. Risk is cases divided by the population at risk. Relative risk compares the risk in an exposed group with the risk in an unexposed group. Absolute risk difference subtracts one risk from the other.','A relative risk of 2 means the exposed group’s risk is twice the comparison group’s risk, but it does not say how large either risk is. Doubling a risk from 1 in 10,000 to 2 in 10,000 is very different from doubling it from 10% to 20%. Report both absolute and relative measures. Association is not causation: confounders, chance and bias must be considered before concluding that an exposure causes an outcome.',['In a study, 30 of 300 smokers and 10 of 500 non-smokers develop a condition. Calculate each risk and the relative risk.','Risk in smokers is 30/300 = 0.10, and in non-smokers 10/500 = 0.02. The relative risk is 0.10/0.02 = 5, and the absolute difference is 8 percentage points. The association is strong but confounding must still be considered.'],[['Risk','Cases divided by the number of people at risk.',['risk','divid','cases']],['Relative risk','Exposed risk divided by unexposed risk compares the two groups.',['relative','ratio','compar']],['Absolute difference','Subtracting risks shows the size of the real-world change.',['absolute','differ','subtract']]],['Which measure counts only new cases during a time period?',['Incidence','Prevalence','Median','Range'],'Incidence describes new cases; prevalence includes existing cases.'],['If risk is 0.06 in an exposed group and 0.03 in an unexposed group, what is the relative risk?',['2','0.03','3','0.5'],'0.06 divided by 0.03 equals 2.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Quality, governance, privacy and ethics','Data integrity and quality are 8% of the test plan, data governance 12%, privacy and security 12%, and ethics 4%. Together they ask whether data is trustworthy, who is responsible for it, and how it is protected.',[
  ['Data quality and governance','Data quality has several dimensions: accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness and validity. Data governance is the framework of policies, roles and decision rights that keeps data trustworthy across an organization. A data steward is responsible for definitions and quality in a domain. A data dictionary defines each field, its format and its allowed values so everyone records and interprets data the same way.','Most quality problems start upstream. If one clinic records weight in pounds and another in kilograms in the same field, downstream reports will be wrong no matter how good the analysis is. Validation rules, such as rejecting a birth date in the future, catch some errors at entry. Governance decides who may change a definition, how master patient index duplicates are resolved, and how long records are retained.',['A dashboard shows an impossible average patient weight of 140 kg in one clinic. What data quality and governance steps should follow?','Check whether units were mixed in the weight field, correct the source data, and update the data dictionary and entry validation so units are explicit. A data steward should own the definition so the error does not return.'],[['Accuracy','Values must correctly represent the real-world fact.',['accura','correct','real']],['Data dictionary','Defines each field’s meaning, format and allowed values.',['dictionary','defin','format']],['Data steward','A named owner keeps definitions and quality consistent.',['steward','owner','responsib']]],['Which tool defines each field’s meaning, format and allowed values?',['Data dictionary','Firewall','Screen saver','Fax cover sheet'],'A data dictionary documents field definitions so data is recorded consistently.'],['A birth date in the future is rejected at entry. Which quality dimension does this protect?',['Validity','Timeliness only','Font size','Storage cost'],'Validation rules check that values fall within allowed limits.']],
  ['Privacy, security and ethical use','In the United States the HIPAA Privacy Rule governs how covered entities and business associates use and disclose protected health information. Patients have rights to access their records and request corrections. The minimum necessary standard limits use to what a task requires. The Security Rule requires administrative, physical and technical safeguards for electronic information, such as risk analysis, access controls, audit logs and encryption.','Privacy is about appropriate use; security is about protection from unauthorized access. An employee looking up a celebrity’s chart without a work reason is a privacy violation even though no hacker was involved. Role-based access, audit trails and training reduce this risk. Ethical use goes further than legal compliance: de-identified data can still harm groups if it is used to deny services, and algorithms trained on biased data can reproduce inequities.',['A staff member who does not treat a well-known patient opens the patient’s record out of curiosity. Explain what principle was violated and how organizations detect it.','The access violated the minimum necessary standard and patient privacy because there was no treatment, payment or operations reason. Audit logs record who opened which record, and routine audit review can detect the access and trigger corrective action.'],[['Minimum necessary','Use only the information a task actually requires.',['minimum','necessary','need']],['Audit trail','Logs show who accessed which record and when.',['audit','log','track']],['Privacy versus security','Privacy governs appropriate use; security protects against unauthorized access.',['privacy','security','protect']]],['Which safeguard is technical under the HIPAA Security Rule?',['Encryption and access controls','A locked filing cabinet only','A training calendar','A visitor badge'],'Encryption and access controls are technical safeguards for electronic data.'],['An employee views a chart with no work reason. What is this?',['A privacy violation','Appropriate treatment use','A data dictionary update','Interoperability'],'Access without a legitimate purpose violates patient privacy even without an outside attacker.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Research design and emerging technology','Research study and design is 12% of the test plan and emerging technologies 4%. Expect to judge study design, recognize bias and interpret what a result can and cannot show.',[
  ['Study designs and bias','Observational studies watch what happens without assigning exposures. Cross-sectional studies measure exposure and outcome at one time. Case-control studies start with people who have an outcome and compare past exposures with controls. Cohort studies follow exposed and unexposed groups forward. Randomized controlled trials assign interventions by chance, which balances known and unknown confounders on average, and are the strongest single design for testing cause and effect.','Bias is systematic error. Selection bias arises when the people studied differ from the population of interest. Information bias arises from inaccurate measurement, such as people with a disease recalling exposures more carefully. Confounding happens when a third factor is linked to both exposure and outcome. Blinding, randomization, careful sampling and validated measurements reduce bias; a larger sample reduces random error but does not fix bias.',['Researchers find coffee drinkers have more lung cancer. Before concluding coffee causes cancer, what should they check?','Smoking may be a confounder because it is associated with both coffee drinking and lung cancer. They should measure smoking and adjust for it, or use a design that controls for it, before inferring causation.'],[['Randomization','Assigning by chance balances confounders across groups on average.',['random','chance','balanc']],['Confounding','A third factor linked to exposure and outcome distorts the association.',['confound','third','smok']],['Selection bias','The study sample differs systematically from the target population.',['selection','sample','represent']]],['Which design starts with people who already have the outcome?',['Case-control study','Randomized controlled trial','Cohort study','Cross-over trial'],'Case-control studies compare past exposures between cases and controls.'],['Which statement about sample size is correct?',['Larger samples reduce random error but do not remove bias','Larger samples always remove bias','Sample size never matters','Small samples prevent confounding'],'Bias is systematic and must be addressed through design and measurement.']],
  ['Emerging technologies and informatics ethics','Emerging technologies include artificial intelligence for image analysis and risk prediction, telehealth, remote patient monitoring, wearable sensors and natural language processing of clinical notes. Clinical decision support presents alerts, order sets or predictions at the point of care. These tools can widen access and catch problems early, but they depend on the quality and representativeness of their data.','A prediction model is only as good as its validation. A sepsis alert trained at one hospital may perform worse at another with a different population or documentation habits. Too many low-value alerts cause alert fatigue, so clinicians begin ignoring warnings. Ethical deployment asks who benefits, who might be harmed, whether patients understand how their data is used, and how humans stay accountable for decisions.',['A hospital adopts an AI tool that flags skin lesions. It performs well on light skin but misses lesions on darker skin. Explain the cause and the responsible response.','The training data probably under-represented darker skin tones, so the model did not generalize. The hospital should evaluate performance across groups before and after deployment, retrain or limit use, and keep clinicians accountable for final decisions.'],[['Training data','Models learn patterns from the data they are given.',['train','data','represent']],['Validation','Performance must be tested in the population where the tool is used.',['validat','test','perform']],['Accountability','Clinicians and organizations remain responsible for decisions.',['accountab','responsib','human']]],['What is alert fatigue?',['Clinicians ignoring frequent low-value alerts','A battery running low','A patient tired from testing','Network latency'],'Excessive alerts desensitize users so important warnings may be missed.'],['Why should an AI model be evaluated across patient groups?',['Performance can differ when groups are under-represented in training data','Models never differ across groups','It is required only for paper records','It increases screen brightness'],'Uneven training data can produce uneven accuracy and inequitable care.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Healthcare Administration',[{title:'AHRQ: quality and patient safety',url:'https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/index.html'},{title:'WHO: health systems',url:'https://www.who.int/health-topics/health-systems-governance'}],[
 ['Unit 1 · Health systems and leadership','Globalization of delivery systems, leadership and management, and organization design each account for 10% of the Healthcare Administration test plan. The test plan is evenly weighted across ten domains, so steady coverage matters more than depth in any one area.',[
  ['Comparing health care delivery systems','Countries organize and finance care differently. In single-payer models one public payer funds most care. Social insurance models use mandatory contributions to non-profit funds. Mixed systems, such as the United States, combine employer-based private insurance, public programs like Medicare and Medicaid, and out-of-pocket payment. Every system balances three pressures: cost, access and quality.','Improving one pressure often strains another. Expanding coverage increases access but may raise total spending unless prices or use are managed. Global challenges such as aging populations, chronic disease, workforce shortages and new technologies affect every system. When comparing countries, use measures such as life expectancy, infant mortality, spending per person and avoidable mortality, and remember that health outcomes also depend on social conditions outside medical care.',['A country expands insurance to millions of uninsured residents. Predict two effects on the system and one risk administrators should plan for.','Access improves and use of preventive care likely rises. Total spending may increase. Administrators should plan for workforce and appointment capacity so new coverage translates into real access rather than longer waits.'],[['Cost','Total spending and prices affect affordability for patients and payers.',['cost','spend','afford']],['Access','People must be able to obtain timely care when they need it.',['access','timely','wait']],['Quality','Care should be safe, effective and patient-centered.',['quality','safe','effective']]],['Which three pressures are often called the iron triangle of health care?',['Cost, access and quality','Height, weight and age','Doctors, nurses and pharmacists','Buildings, parking and signage'],'Health systems constantly balance cost, access and quality.'],['Which U.S. program primarily covers adults 65 and older?',['Medicare','Medicaid only for children','Workers’ compensation','A health savings account'],'Medicare is the federal program for people 65 and older and some younger people with disabilities.']],
  ['Leadership, management and organization design','Management plans, organizes, staffs, directs and controls resources to reach goals. Leadership influences people toward a vision. Situational leadership adapts the leader’s style to a follower’s competence and commitment. Transformational leaders inspire change through vision and personal example. Organization design arranges reporting relationships and coordination: functional structures group similar work, service lines organize around patient populations, and matrix structures combine both.','Structure should follow strategy. A hospital that wants to grow cardiac care may create a cardiovascular service line so clinicians, budget and quality measures are coordinated around those patients. Span of control describes how many people report to one manager; a wide span allows autonomy but limits coaching time. Coordination mechanisms such as standardized protocols, huddles and liaison roles reduce the gaps where errors and delays occur.',['A new nurse is enthusiastic but inexperienced, while a senior nurse is highly skilled but disengaged. How should a situational leader adjust?','Give the new nurse clear direction and close support while skills develop. Give the senior nurse more autonomy and involvement in decisions to rebuild commitment. The style follows each person’s competence and commitment.'],[['Situational leadership','Leadership style adapts to each follower’s competence and commitment.',['situation','adapt','competen']],['Service line','Resources are organized around a patient population or condition.',['service','line','population']],['Span of control','The number of direct reports shapes oversight and autonomy.',['span','report','oversight']]],['What is a service line in a health organization?',['An organization of resources around a patient population or condition','A telephone queue','The cafeteria serving line','A billing code'],'Service lines coordinate staff, budgets and quality around a type of care.'],['Which leadership approach adjusts style to the employee’s needs and skills?',['Situational leadership','Autocratic leadership in all cases','Laissez-faire only','Seniority-based leadership'],'Situational leadership changes direction and support based on the follower.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · People, teams and communication','Motivating people and conflict management, teams and team effectiveness, and communication are each 10% of the test plan. These domains test how administrators get work done through others.',[
  ['Motivation and conflict management','Motivation theories explain why people work. Maslow’s hierarchy proposes needs from physiological to self-actualization. Herzberg separated hygiene factors, such as pay and working conditions, which prevent dissatisfaction, from motivators, such as recognition and growth, which build satisfaction. Expectancy theory says effort depends on believing effort leads to performance, performance leads to rewards, and rewards are valued.','Conflict is not always harmful: disagreement about tasks can improve decisions, while personal conflict tends to damage teams. Common approaches include avoiding, accommodating, competing, compromising and collaborating. Collaboration seeks a solution that meets both parties’ core interests and is worth the time when the issue matters and the relationship continues. A manager should separate positions from interests: two units arguing over a room may both really need predictable time for procedures.',['Two departments argue over a shared procedure room. How would a collaborating manager approach the conflict?','The manager meets both departments, identifies their underlying interests such as predictable time and equipment readiness, and builds a shared schedule that meets both. Collaborating focuses on interests rather than who wins the room.'],[['Hygiene factors','Pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate alone.',['hygiene','pay','dissatisf']],['Motivators','Recognition, growth and meaningful work build satisfaction.',['recognit','growth','motivat']],['Collaboration','Finding solutions that meet both parties’ underlying interests.',['collaborat','interest','both']]],['In Herzberg’s theory, salary is best classified as a:',['Hygiene factor','Motivator','Self-actualization need','Team norm'],'Pay prevents dissatisfaction but is not considered a primary motivator.'],['Which conflict style seeks a solution that fully satisfies both parties?',['Collaborating','Avoiding','Competing','Accommodating'],'Collaborating pursues mutual gains rather than a split or a winner.']],
  ['Team effectiveness and communication','Effective teams have a clear purpose, defined roles, mutual accountability and psychological safety, meaning members feel safe to speak up. Tuckman described stages of team development: forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning. Groupthink occurs when the desire for agreement overrides critical evaluation. In health care, structured tools improve communication: SBAR organizes a message as situation, background, assessment and recommendation, and closed-loop communication confirms that a message was received.','Communication failures are a leading contributor to serious adverse events, especially during handoffs. Administrators communicate through many channels; rich, two-way channels such as face-to-face conversation suit complex or sensitive messages, while written channels suit routine information that needs a record. Leaders who invite dissent, rotate a devil’s advocate role and ask quieter members first reduce groupthink.',['A team agrees quickly to a new discharge process, and no one raises concerns. Two weeks later readmissions rise. What might have happened and how could a leader prevent it?','The team may have experienced groupthink, accepting the plan without critical evaluation. The leader could invite dissent, assign someone to challenge the plan, and pilot the process with measures before full rollout.'],[['Psychological safety','Members can raise concerns without fear of blame.',['safety','speak','concern']],['Groupthink','Desire for agreement suppresses critical thinking.',['groupthink','agree','critic']],['SBAR','Situation, background, assessment and recommendation structure a message.',['sbar','situation','recommend']]],['What does the B in SBAR stand for?',['Background','Blood pressure','Benefit','Budget'],'SBAR stands for situation, background, assessment and recommendation.'],['Which stage of team development involves conflict over roles and approaches?',['Storming','Norming','Performing','Adjourning'],'Storming is the stage in which disagreements surface.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Quality and strategy','Improving quality and strategy and achieving mission each represent 10% of the test plan. These domains link daily operations to measurable results.',[
  ['Measuring and improving quality','Donabedian’s model evaluates quality through structure, process and outcome. Structure is the setting and resources, process is what is done, and outcome is the result for patients. The Institute of Medicine described care that is safe, effective, patient-centered, timely, efficient and equitable. The Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle tests small changes, measures the result and adapts before scaling.','Measures must match the question. Hand hygiene compliance is a process measure; central line infection rate is an outcome measure; the number of sinks is a structure measure. Lean methods remove waste from processes, and Six Sigma reduces variation. Root cause analysis examines why an adverse event happened, focusing on systems rather than blaming individuals, because most errors arise from flawed processes rather than careless people.',['A unit wants to reduce patient falls. Give one structure, one process and one outcome measure and describe a first PDSA cycle.','Structure: bed alarms available on every bed. Process: percentage of patients with a documented fall-risk assessment. Outcome: falls per 1,000 patient days. A first PDSA cycle might test hourly rounding on one shift for two weeks, study fall and rounding data, then adjust before spreading.'],[['Structure','Resources and setting in which care occurs.',['structure','resource','equipment']],['Process','The actions taken while delivering care.',['process','assess','round']],['Outcome','The result of care for patients.',['outcome','falls','result']]],['Infection rate after surgery is which type of measure?',['Outcome','Structure','Process','Input only'],'It measures a result for patients.'],['What does PDSA stand for?',['Plan-Do-Study-Act','Patient-Doctor-Staff-Administrator','Plan-Decide-Spend-Audit','Prepare-Deliver-Sign-Archive'],'PDSA is an iterative improvement cycle.']],
  ['Strategy and achieving mission','A mission statement explains why an organization exists, a vision describes the future it seeks, and values guide behavior. Strategic planning analyzes the environment, sets goals and allocates resources. A SWOT analysis lists internal strengths and weaknesses and external opportunities and threats. A balanced scorecard tracks performance across financial, customer, internal process and learning perspectives so leaders do not focus on money alone.','Strategy turns choices into action. Goals should be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. A community hospital with a mission to serve its rural population might choose telehealth partnerships instead of building an expensive specialty wing. Strategic decisions should be monitored with leading indicators, which predict future results, and lagging indicators, which confirm past results.',['A rural hospital loses patients to a city hospital for cardiology visits. Use SWOT reasoning to suggest a strategy aligned with its mission.','A strength may be community trust and a weakness limited specialists. The opportunity is telehealth partnerships; the threat is losing patients to the city. A telecardiology clinic with visiting specialists keeps care local and supports the mission.'],[['Mission','Defines why the organization exists and whom it serves.',['mission','purpose','serve']],['SWOT analysis','Internal strengths and weaknesses with external opportunities and threats.',['swot','strength','threat']],['Balanced scorecard','Tracks financial, customer, process and learning measures together.',['scorecard','balanced','measure']]],['In a SWOT analysis, a new competitor opening nearby is a:',['Threat','Strength','Weakness','Value'],'Competitors are external threats.'],['Which describes why an organization exists?',['Mission statement','Payroll register','Floor plan','Incident report'],'The mission statement states purpose.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Policy, regulation, consumerism and ethics','Health policy and regulation, and consumerism and ethics, each make up 10% of the test plan. Administrators work within laws, accreditation standards and rising patient expectations.',[
  ['Health policy and regulation','Health policy is shaped by legislatures, agencies and courts. Key U.S. agencies include the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, which administers public insurance programs, the Food and Drug Administration, which regulates drugs and devices, and the Occupational Safety and Health Administration, which protects workers. Accrediting bodies such as The Joint Commission survey hospitals against standards. EMTALA requires hospitals with emergency departments to screen and stabilize anyone who seeks emergency care regardless of ability to pay.','Regulation aims to protect safety, quality and fair access, but compliance has costs. Payment policy also shapes behavior: fee-for-service pays for each service, which may encourage volume, while value-based payment rewards outcomes and efficiency. Administrators track rule changes, train staff, document compliance and report required data. Violations may bring fines, loss of program participation or reputational harm.',['An uninsured patient arrives at an emergency department with chest pain. What does EMTALA require?','The hospital must provide a medical screening examination and stabilizing treatment within its capability, or an appropriate transfer, regardless of the patient’s ability to pay. Delaying screening to ask about insurance would violate the law.'],[['EMTALA','Emergency screening and stabilization regardless of ability to pay.',['emtala','screen','stabiliz']],['Value-based payment','Payment tied to outcomes and efficiency rather than volume.',['value','outcome','payment']],['Accreditation','Independent survey against quality and safety standards.',['accredit','survey','standard']]],['Which agency administers Medicare and Medicaid?',['Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services','Food and Drug Administration','Federal Aviation Administration','Environmental Protection Agency'],'CMS administers these programs.'],['Fee-for-service payment tends to reward:',['Volume of services','Population health only','Fewer visits','Shared savings'],'Paying for each service rewards doing more services.']],
  ['Consumerism and ethics in administration','Patients increasingly act as health care consumers, comparing prices, ratings and convenience. Price transparency rules require hospitals to publish standard charges. Patient experience surveys influence reputation and, in some programs, payment. Administrative ethics apply principles such as respect for autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice to organizational choices, including how scarce resources are allocated.','Ethical conflicts in administration often involve competing goods: closing an unprofitable service may protect the organization’s finances but reduce access for a vulnerable community. Conflicts of interest, such as accepting gifts from vendors, can bias purchasing. Ethics committees, transparent criteria and stakeholder engagement help leaders make defensible decisions. Marketing must be truthful; advertising outcomes that are not representative misleads consumers.',['A hospital considers closing its obstetrics unit because it loses money. What ethical considerations should administrators weigh?','They should weigh financial sustainability against access for pregnant patients, especially if the nearest alternative is far away. Justice and non-maleficence call for assessing community impact, exploring partnerships, and communicating transparently before deciding.'],[['Justice','Fair distribution of benefits and burdens across the community.',['justice','fair','access']],['Conflict of interest','Personal gain that could bias an organizational decision.',['conflict','interest','gift']],['Transparency','Clear, honest communication about prices, criteria and decisions.',['transparen','honest','communicat']]],['Which principle concerns fair distribution of resources?',['Justice','Autonomy','Fidelity to a vendor','Efficiency only'],'Justice addresses fairness in allocating benefits and burdens.'],['A purchasing manager accepts a vacation from a supplier under evaluation. This is:',['A conflict of interest','Price transparency','Value-based purchasing','Accreditation'],'The gift could bias the purchasing decision.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Human Growth & Development',[OS('lifespan-development','Lifespan Development'),OS('anatomy-and-physiology-2e','Anatomy and Physiology 2e')],[
 ['Unit 1 · Influences and theories','Government, cultural and family influences make up 20% of the Human Growth & Development test plan and theories of development 16%. Development is shaped by biology and environment working together across the whole lifespan.',[
  ['Family, culture and policy as developmental contexts','Bronfenbrenner’s ecological systems theory places the person within nested systems: the microsystem of family, school and peers; the mesosystem of connections among them; the exosystem, such as a parent’s workplace; the macrosystem of culture, laws and values; and the chronosystem of change over time. Parenting styles are often described as authoritative, authoritarian, permissive and uninvolved. Government policy influences development through nutrition programs, education, child labor laws and health insurance.','The same behavior can carry different meanings across cultures, so caregivers and health workers should ask about family practices rather than assume. Authoritative parenting, which pairs warmth with clear expectations, is associated with positive outcomes in many studies, though cultural context matters. Poverty affects development through nutrition, stress, housing and access to services. Programs such as WIC aim to support pregnant people, infants and young children with nutrition and referrals.',['A parent loses a job, and the family moves to a smaller apartment in a new school district. Using ecological systems theory, explain how this may affect a child.','A change in the exosystem (the parent’s workplace) and macrosystem conditions such as economic policy alters the microsystem through financial stress and a new school and peers. The mesosystem links between home and school must also be rebuilt.'],[['Microsystem','Direct relationships such as family, school and peers.',['micro','family','school']],['Exosystem','Settings that affect the child indirectly, like a parent’s job.',['exo','parent','job']],['Macrosystem','Culture, laws, values and economic conditions.',['macro','culture','policy']]],['Which parenting style pairs warmth with clear, reasonable expectations?',['Authoritative','Authoritarian','Permissive','Uninvolved'],'Authoritative parenting combines responsiveness and structure.'],['In Bronfenbrenner’s model, cultural values and laws belong to the:',['Macrosystem','Microsystem','Mesosystem','Chronosystem only'],'The macrosystem contains broad cultural and societal influences.']],
  ['Major developmental theories','Piaget described cognitive stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Erikson described eight psychosocial crises across life, from trust versus mistrust in infancy to integrity versus despair in late adulthood. Freud proposed psychosexual stages. Kohlberg described levels of moral reasoning. Vygotsky emphasized social learning and the zone of proximal development, the gap between what a learner can do alone and with guidance.','Theories are tools for organizing observations, not rules every person follows on schedule. Piaget focuses on how thinking changes; Erikson on social and emotional challenges; Vygotsky on how culture and guidance shape learning. A health worker can apply them practically: explaining a procedure concretely to a seven-year-old fits concrete operational thinking, and involving an adolescent in decisions supports the identity crisis Erikson described.',['A 7-year-old asks why the nurse needs a blood sample. How should the explanation be adapted using Piaget’s theory?','A child in the concrete operational stage understands real, tangible explanations. Show the equipment, use simple concrete terms, and describe exactly what they will feel and see rather than abstract explanations.'],[['Piaget','Cognitive development moves through qualitatively different stages.',['piaget','cognit','concrete']],['Erikson','Each life stage centers on a psychosocial crisis.',['erikson','crisis','identity']],['Zone of proximal development','Tasks a learner can achieve with guidance.',['proximal','guidance','vygotsky']]],['According to Erikson, which crisis is central in adolescence?',['Identity versus role confusion','Trust versus mistrust','Integrity versus despair','Industry versus inferiority'],'Adolescents work to form a stable sense of identity.'],['In which Piaget stage does abstract, hypothetical reasoning typically emerge?',['Formal operational','Sensorimotor','Preoperational','Concrete operational'],'Formal operational thinking includes abstract reasoning.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Prenatal development through adolescence','Prenatal and infancy make up 12% of the test plan, and childhood and adolescence 22%, the largest single domain. Expect questions about milestones, growth and health risks at each stage.',[
  ['Prenatal development and infancy','Prenatal development has three periods: the germinal period of about two weeks after fertilization, the embryonic period through about week eight when organs form, and the fetal period of growth and maturation until birth. Teratogens are agents such as alcohol, some infections and certain medications that can cause birth defects; the embryonic period is especially sensitive. After birth, infants show reflexes such as rooting, sucking and the Moro reflex, and gain motor skills in a head-to-toe and center-outward pattern.','Milestones are typical ranges, not deadlines. Most infants sit without support around six months and walk around twelve months, but healthy variation is wide. Attachment develops through responsive caregiving; secure attachment supports later emotional regulation. Health workers support infant development through prenatal care, safe sleep guidance such as placing infants on their backs, immunizations and screening for developmental delays.',['A pregnant patient in week five asks whether occasional alcohol matters. Explain why timing is important.','Week five is in the embryonic period, when organs are forming and the embryo is especially sensitive to teratogens. No amount of alcohol is known to be safe in pregnancy, and exposure can cause lifelong effects, so avoiding alcohol is recommended.'],[['Embryonic period','Organs form, making this a highly sensitive stage.',['embryo','organ','sensitiv']],['Teratogens','Agents that can disrupt development and cause defects.',['teratogen','alcohol','defect']],['Cephalocaudal pattern','Development proceeds from head to toe.',['head','cephalo','toe']]],['During which prenatal period do major organs form?',['Embryonic period','Germinal period','Fetal period only','After birth'],'The embryonic period, roughly weeks 3 through 8, is when organs form.'],['Which reflex causes an infant to turn toward a touch on the cheek?',['Rooting reflex','Babinski reflex','Patellar reflex','Moro reflex'],'Rooting helps an infant locate the nipple for feeding.']],
  ['Childhood and adolescence','Early childhood brings rapid language growth and pretend play. Middle childhood brings logical thinking, competence in school and refined motor coordination. Adolescence begins with puberty, driven by hormonal changes that produce growth spurts and secondary sex characteristics. The prefrontal cortex, which supports planning and impulse control, continues maturing into the mid-twenties while emotional reward systems mature earlier.','This uneven brain timing helps explain why adolescents may take more risks with peers present even when they understand the dangers. Common health concerns include injuries, obesity, mental health conditions, substance use and sexual health. Confidential, respectful conversations and anticipatory guidance help adolescents make informed choices. In childhood, routine well visits track growth on percentile charts, where a sudden change in percentile matters more than a single value.',['A 15-year-old understands the risks of texting while driving but still does it when friends are in the car. Explain this using adolescent brain development.','Emotional and reward systems mature before the prefrontal cortex, which supports impulse control. With peers present, reward sensitivity rises, so knowledge alone may not prevent risky choices. Structured rules and supportive guidance help.'],[['Puberty','Hormonal changes drive growth and sexual maturation.',['puberty','hormon','growth']],['Prefrontal cortex','Supports planning and impulse control and matures into the twenties.',['prefrontal','impulse','plan']],['Growth percentiles','Tracking trends on growth charts reveals concerning changes.',['percentile','chart','trend']]],['Which brain region involved in impulse control matures last?',['Prefrontal cortex','Brainstem','Cerebellum','Medulla'],'The prefrontal cortex continues maturing into early adulthood.'],['On a growth chart, which finding is most concerning?',['A sudden drop across two major percentile lines','A steady 40th percentile','Height and weight both stable','Normal variation within one line'],'Crossing percentile lines suggests a change in growth that needs evaluation.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Adulthood','Adulthood accounts for 15% of the test plan. Development continues through careers, relationships, parenting and the gradual physical changes of middle age.',[
  ['Early and middle adulthood','Early adulthood typically involves establishing independence, careers and close relationships; Erikson framed this as intimacy versus isolation. Physical capacity often peaks in the twenties. Middle adulthood brings generativity versus stagnation, as people focus on guiding the next generation and contributing to society. Physical changes include presbyopia, gradual loss of muscle mass, and for women menopause, the end of menstrual periods confirmed after twelve months without menstruation.','Health habits in early and middle adulthood strongly influence later life. Screening such as blood pressure, cholesterol, diabetes and age-appropriate cancer screenings detects disease early. Midlife is often described as a sandwich period when adults care for both children and aging parents, which can bring stress. Cognitive skills based on knowledge and experience, called crystallized intelligence, often stay stable or grow, while processing speed, part of fluid intelligence, gradually slows.',['A 48-year-old is caring for teenagers and an aging parent and reports exhaustion. What developmental and health factors should a health worker consider?','The patient is in the sandwich generation of middle adulthood with high caregiving demands. Assess stress, sleep and mental health, review screening for chronic disease, and connect the patient with respite or support resources.'],[['Generativity','Contributing to the next generation and society.',['generativ','guid','contribut']],['Crystallized intelligence','Knowledge and experience that remain stable or grow.',['crystal','knowledge','experience']],['Presbyopia','Age-related difficulty focusing on near objects.',['presbyop','near','focus']]],['Erikson’s crisis of middle adulthood is:',['Generativity versus stagnation','Trust versus mistrust','Initiative versus guilt','Identity versus role confusion'],'Middle adults focus on contributing to others.'],['Menopause is confirmed after how long without a menstrual period?',['12 months','1 month','3 weeks','5 years'],'Twelve consecutive months without menstruation confirms menopause.']],
  ['Health promotion across adulthood','Health promotion aims to prevent disease and maintain function. Primary prevention prevents disease before it starts, such as vaccination and exercise. Secondary prevention detects disease early, such as screening mammography. Tertiary prevention limits complications of existing disease, such as cardiac rehabilitation. Lifestyle factors including physical activity, nutrition, sleep, tobacco avoidance and limiting alcohol have large effects on chronic disease risk.','Behavior change is a process. The transtheoretical model describes stages: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance. Matching advice to a person’s stage works better than giving every patient the same plan. A smoker in precontemplation may benefit from information and reflection, while one in preparation needs a quit date and practical tools. Social support and the environment, such as safe places to walk, also shape behavior.',['A 35-year-old smoker says they might quit sometime in the next six months. Identify the stage of change and an appropriate response.','This is the contemplation stage. Help the person weigh benefits and barriers, strengthen their reasons for quitting, and offer information about support options to move toward preparation.'],[['Primary prevention','Preventing disease before it develops.',['primary','prevent','vaccin']],['Secondary prevention','Early detection through screening.',['secondary','screen','early']],['Stages of change','Readiness to change guides the type of support offered.',['stage','contemplat','ready']]],['A mammogram to find early breast cancer is which level of prevention?',['Secondary','Primary','Tertiary','Quaternary only'],'Screening detects existing disease early.'],['A person who has set a quit date next week is in which stage?',['Preparation','Precontemplation','Maintenance','Contemplation'],'Preparation involves planning specific action soon.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Late adulthood, death and grief','Geriatrics, end of life and grief make up 15% of the test plan. Aging is diverse: chronological age predicts less than health, function and social connection.',[
  ['Physical and cognitive aging','Normal aging includes reduced skin elasticity, age spots, slower reaction time, decreased muscle mass, reduced kidney function and changes in vision and hearing. Age-related eye conditions include cataracts, which cloud the lens, glaucoma, which damages the optic nerve, and macular degeneration, which causes loss of central vision. Dementia is not a normal part of aging; it is a decline in memory and thinking severe enough to interfere with daily life.','Distinguishing normal aging from disease matters for care. Forgetting a name and recalling it later is typical; getting lost in a familiar neighborhood is not. Delirium is an acute, fluctuating confusion often caused by infection, medication or dehydration and is a medical emergency, whereas dementia develops gradually. Older adults are vulnerable to falls, polypharmacy and social isolation. Assessing activities of daily living helps plan support that preserves independence.',['An 82-year-old becomes confused over two days after starting a new medication. Is this more likely dementia or delirium, and why?','The sudden onset over days and a new medication suggest delirium, which is acute and often reversible. It needs prompt medical evaluation rather than being dismissed as aging or dementia.'],[['Delirium','Acute, fluctuating confusion with an identifiable cause.',['delirium','acute','sudden']],['Dementia','Gradual decline in cognition interfering with daily life.',['dementia','gradual','decline']],['Macular degeneration','Loss of central vision from macular damage.',['macular','central','vision']]],['Which eye disease causes loss of central vision?',['Macular degeneration','Cataracts only','Presbyopia','Myopia'],'Macular degeneration damages the macula responsible for central vision.'],['Which finding most suggests delirium rather than dementia?',['Sudden onset over hours to days','Slow decline over years','Stable memory loss','Gradual word-finding difficulty'],'Delirium begins abruptly and fluctuates.']],
  ['End of life, grief and loss','Palliative care relieves symptoms and supports quality of life for anyone with serious illness, alongside curative treatment. Hospice is palliative care for people expected to live about six months or less who choose comfort-focused care. Advance directives, such as a living will and a health care power of attorney, record wishes and name a decision-maker. Kübler-Ross described denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance in response to dying.','Grief is individual and does not follow a fixed sequence; people may move back and forth among responses. Cultural and religious beliefs shape rituals, communication preferences and decisions about care. Health workers support dying patients and families by managing pain and breathlessness, listening, respecting wishes and offering bereavement resources. Prolonged grief that severely disrupts life for an extended period may need professional support.',['A family says their grandmother has decided to stop curative cancer treatment, and they feel she is giving up. How should a health worker respond?','Explain that choosing comfort-focused care is a valid decision that respects her autonomy. Palliative or hospice care actively manages symptoms and supports the family. Listening to their grief and connecting them with support helps them adjust.'],[['Palliative care','Symptom relief and quality of life during serious illness.',['palliat','symptom','comfort']],['Advance directive','Documents wishes and names a decision-maker.',['advance','directive','wish']],['Individual grief','Grief responses vary and do not follow a fixed order.',['grief','vary','individual']]],['Hospice care is generally intended for patients with a life expectancy of about:',['Six months or less','Ten years','Two weeks only','Unknown duration with curative intent'],'Hospice eligibility is usually tied to a prognosis of six months or less.'],['Which document names someone to make health decisions if a patient cannot?',['Health care power of attorney','Discharge summary','Lab requisition','Insurance card'],'A health care power of attorney designates a decision-maker.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Medical Law & Ethics',[{title:'HHS: HIPAA for individuals',url:'https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-individuals/index.html'},{title:'AMA Code of Medical Ethics',url:'https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/'}],[
 ['Unit 1 · Foundations and the legal system','The basics of medical law, ethics and bioethics are 5% of the Medical Law & Ethics test plan; the legal environment, including regulations, consent, liability and public duty, is 35%. This course explains general U.S. principles for learning; it is not legal advice, and state laws vary.',[
  ['Law, ethics and bioethics','Law consists of enforceable rules created by legislatures, agencies and courts. Ethics concerns standards of right conduct and may ask more of a professional than the law requires. Bioethics applies ethical reasoning to medicine and life sciences. Four widely used principles are autonomy, respecting a person’s right to make informed choices; beneficence, acting for the patient’s good; non-maleficence, avoiding harm; and justice, fairness in distributing care.','Law and ethics can diverge. Something legal can still be unethical, such as rudely dismissing a patient’s concerns, and an ethical duty such as honesty with a patient may exceed any legal requirement. When principles conflict, professionals weigh them in context: honoring a competent adult’s refusal respects autonomy even when clinicians believe treatment would help. Professional codes of ethics guide behavior, and licensing boards can discipline professionals for unethical conduct.',['A competent adult refuses a blood transfusion for religious reasons. Which principles are in tension, and which generally prevails?','Beneficence suggests transfusion would help, but autonomy protects a competent adult’s informed refusal. In general, a competent adult’s informed refusal is respected, while clinicians explain risks, offer alternatives and document the discussion.'],[['Autonomy','A competent person’s right to make informed choices.',['autonomy','choice','refus']],['Beneficence','Acting to benefit the patient.',['beneficen','benefit','good']],['Law versus ethics','Law is enforceable; ethics may require more than the law.',['law','ethic','enforce']]],['Which principle means “do no harm”?',['Non-maleficence','Beneficence','Justice','Veracity only'],'Non-maleficence is the duty to avoid causing harm.'],['Which statement best describes the relationship between law and ethics?',['Ethical duties can exceed legal requirements','Law and ethics are always identical','Ethics is enforced only by police','Law never addresses health care'],'Professional ethics often asks more than the law requires.']],
  ['Consent, liability and public duty','Informed consent requires disclosure of the nature of a procedure, its risks, benefits and alternatives, given to a competent person who agrees voluntarily. Implied consent applies in emergencies when a patient cannot consent and delay would cause harm. Minors generally need a parent or guardian’s consent, with exceptions in many states for emancipated minors and certain services. Health professionals also have public duties, such as reporting suspected child abuse, certain communicable diseases, births and deaths.','Liability arises when a professional breaches a legal duty. Many claims are torts: intentional torts such as battery, touching without consent, and unintentional torts such as negligence. Respondeat superior holds employers liable for employees’ acts within the scope of employment. Mandatory reporting overrides confidentiality when the law requires it; a health worker who suspects child abuse must report even without proof, and good-faith reporters are generally protected.',['A health worker notices injuries on a child that do not match the parent’s explanation. What is the legal duty?','Health workers are mandated reporters. The worker must report suspected abuse to the appropriate authority according to policy and state law; proof is not required, and good-faith reports are generally protected.'],[['Informed consent','Disclosure, competence and voluntary agreement.',['consent','disclos','risk']],['Battery','Touching a person without consent.',['battery','touch','without']],['Mandatory reporting','Legal duty to report certain conditions such as suspected abuse.',['report','mandat','abuse']]],['Performing a procedure without the patient’s consent can be classified as:',['Battery','Libel','Slander','Respondeat superior'],'Unconsented touching is battery.'],['What is required before a health worker reports suspected child abuse?',['Reasonable suspicion','Proof beyond reasonable doubt','A court order','The parent’s permission'],'Reporting is based on reasonable suspicion, not proof.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · The health care environment','The health care environment is 35% of the test plan: physician-patient relationships, malpractice, records, workplace law, cultural perspective, confidentiality and employment practices.',[
  ['The patient relationship and malpractice','A physician-patient relationship forms a contract, often implied, that creates a duty of care. Abandonment occurs when a provider ends care without reasonable notice or arranging a substitute while the patient still needs care. Malpractice is professional negligence. A negligence claim requires the four Ds: duty, dereliction or breach of duty, direct cause, and damages. The standard of care is what a reasonably prudent professional with similar training would do in similar circumstances.','All four elements must be shown; an error that causes no harm usually does not support a malpractice award, though it should still be reported and learned from. Res ipsa loquitur, “the thing speaks for itself,” applies when harm would not normally occur without negligence, such as a surgical instrument left inside a patient. Risk management, clear documentation, honest disclosure of errors and staying within one’s scope of practice reduce both harm and liability.',['A medical assistant gives a medication outside their scope, and the patient has a serious reaction. Analyze the case using the four Ds.','Duty existed because the assistant was caring for the patient. Dereliction occurred by acting outside scope and the standard of care. The action directly caused the reaction, and the patient suffered damages. All four elements support negligence, and the employer may also be liable.'],[['Duty','A care relationship creates a legal obligation.',['duty','relationship','obligat']],['Dereliction','Failing to meet the standard of care.',['derelict','breach','standard']],['Direct cause and damages','The breach must cause actual harm.',['cause','damage','harm']]],['Which list correctly names the four Ds of negligence?',['Duty, dereliction, direct cause, damages','Diagnosis, drugs, discharge, death','Delay, denial, defense, discovery','Documentation, disclosure, duty, dismissal'],'All four elements are needed for a negligence claim.'],['A surgical sponge left in a patient is an example of:',['Res ipsa loquitur','Implied consent','Good Samaritan protection','Emancipation'],'The harm would not normally occur without negligence.']],
  ['Records, confidentiality and the workplace','The medical record is a legal document belonging to the provider or facility, while patients have rights to access and copy their information. Corrections should be made by drawing a single line through the error, writing the correction, and dating and initialing it, or by a tracked amendment in electronic systems; never erase or delete. Confidentiality protects patient information; HIPAA permits disclosure for treatment, payment and operations, and otherwise generally requires authorization unless an exception such as mandatory reporting applies.','Workplace law protects both workers and patients. Equal employment laws prohibit discrimination based on protected characteristics, and the Americans with Disabilities Act requires reasonable accommodations. OSHA standards, such as the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard, protect workers from occupational hazards. Cultural humility, which means recognizing one’s own assumptions and learning from each patient, supports respectful care and fewer misunderstandings.',['A friend calls a clinic asking whether a neighbor was seen that day. How should staff respond?','Staff should not confirm or deny that the neighbor is a patient without authorization, because that would disclose protected health information. They can politely explain that they cannot share patient information.'],[['Record correction','Single line, correction, date and initials; never erase.',['correct','line','initial']],['Confidentiality','Patient information is shared only when permitted.',['confiden','permit','authoriz']],['Workplace protection','Laws like OSHA and the ADA protect workers.',['osha','ada','worker']]],['How should an error in a paper medical record be corrected?',['Draw a single line through it, correct it, date and initial it','Use correction fluid','Tear out the page','Erase it carefully'],'Original entries must stay readable for legal integrity.'],['Which agency sets standards protecting workers from bloodborne pathogens?',['OSHA','FDA','FAA','USDA'],'OSHA enforces the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Bioethical issues in medicine','Bioethical issues make up 25% of the test plan, including allocation of scarce resources, genetic engineering, reproductive issues and end-of-life issues. These topics involve reasonable disagreement; strong answers explain the principles and perspectives involved.',[
  ['Allocation of scarce resources and genetics','When demand exceeds supply, as with organs, ICU beds or new drugs, allocation decisions must be fair. Approaches include medical need, likelihood of benefit, first-come first-served, and lottery. Organ allocation in the United States is coordinated through a national network using medical criteria. Genetic testing and engineering raise questions about privacy, discrimination, consent and the line between treatment and enhancement. The Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act limits use of genetic information in health insurance and employment.','Utilitarian reasoning seeks the greatest benefit for the greatest number, while deontological reasoning focuses on duties and rights regardless of outcomes. Allocation by ability to pay or social worth is widely rejected as unjust. In genetics, somatic gene therapy affects only the treated person, whereas germline editing would pass changes to future generations, which raises greater ethical concern. Informed consent for genetic testing should explain implications for relatives.',['A hospital has one ICU bed and two critically ill patients. Explain how fair allocation criteria should guide the decision.','Criteria should be transparent and medically based, such as urgency and likelihood of benefit, applied consistently by a triage process. Ability to pay or social status should not be used. The decision and reasoning should be documented.'],[['Utilitarian reasoning','Seeks the greatest good for the greatest number.',['utilitarian','greatest','benefit']],['Fair criteria','Transparent, medically based and consistently applied.',['fair','criteria','consistent']],['Germline editing','Genetic changes that would pass to future generations.',['germline','future','generation']]],['Which law limits genetic discrimination in health insurance and employment?',['GINA','EMTALA','OSHA','CLIA'],'The Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act addresses genetic discrimination.'],['Which genetic intervention would affect future generations?',['Germline editing','Somatic gene therapy','Genetic counseling','Carrier screening'],'Germline changes are heritable.']],
  ['Reproductive and end-of-life issues','Reproductive ethics includes assisted reproductive technology, surrogacy, prenatal testing and contraception, along with differing legal rules across states. End-of-life issues include advance directives, do-not-resuscitate orders, withholding or withdrawing life-sustaining treatment, and medical aid in dying, which is legal only in some jurisdictions. The Patient Self-Determination Act requires many facilities to inform patients of their right to create advance directives.','Withholding and withdrawing life-sustaining treatment are generally treated as ethically equivalent when consistent with a patient’s informed wishes. When a patient lacks capacity, a surrogate uses substituted judgment, deciding as the patient would have, or the best interest standard when wishes are unknown. Health workers may have conscientious objections to some services but must not abandon patients and should follow policy for transferring care.',['A patient without capacity has a living will refusing mechanical ventilation, but a family member insists on it. How should the team proceed?','The living will expresses the patient’s own wishes and should guide care. The team should communicate compassionately with the family, involve an ethics committee if conflict persists, and follow policy and applicable law.'],[['Advance directive','Written instructions about future medical care.',['advance','directive','living will']],['Substituted judgment','The surrogate decides as the patient would have.',['substitut','surrogate','wish']],['Conscientious objection','Personal objections must not lead to patient abandonment.',['conscien','object','abandon']]],['Which federal law requires many facilities to tell patients about advance directives?',['Patient Self-Determination Act','HIPAA Security Rule','Fair Labor Standards Act','Controlled Substances Act'],'The PSDA requires informing patients of their rights to make advance directives.'],['When a surrogate decides as the patient would have chosen, this is called:',['Substituted judgment','Implied consent','Respondeat superior','Battery'],'Substituted judgment reflects the patient’s known values.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Applying law and ethics to cases','Cases on the test often combine domains. This unit practices structured reasoning so you can identify the legal duty, ethical principles and correct action in unfamiliar situations.',[
  ['Structured ethical decision-making','A structured approach prevents rushed decisions. Identify the facts and stakeholders, name the ethical principles and legal requirements involved, list realistic options, evaluate each against principles, policy and law, choose and act, then document and reflect. Ethics committees provide consultation when conflict persists. Veracity, telling the truth, and fidelity, keeping promises, are additional principles often relevant to cases.','Distinguish what you must do legally from what you should do ethically. A health worker who sees a colleague falsify records has a professional duty to report it through proper channels, protecting patients and the integrity of care. Whistleblower laws protect employees who report illegal practices in good faith. Admitting an error honestly, with the support of risk management, respects veracity and often improves trust.',['A student volunteer sees an employee document vital signs that were never taken. Apply a structured approach to decide what to do.','The facts suggest falsified documentation, which risks patient harm and is illegal and unethical. Options include ignoring it or reporting it. Veracity and non-maleficence require reporting through the supervisor or compliance channel and documenting what was observed.'],[['Identify principles','Name the ethical principles and laws involved.',['principle','law','identify']],['Evaluate options','Compare realistic options against principles and policy.',['option','evaluat','compare']],['Document and reflect','Record the decision and learn from it.',['document','record','reflect']]],['Which principle refers to truthfulness?',['Veracity','Fidelity','Justice','Beneficence'],'Veracity is the duty to tell the truth.'],['Which law protects employees who report illegal practices in good faith?',['Whistleblower protections','Good Samaritan law only','EMTALA','GINA'],'Whistleblower laws protect good-faith reporting.']],
  ['Scope of practice, Good Samaritan laws and licensure','Scope of practice defines the procedures and actions a professional is permitted to perform under licensure or certification and state law. Licensure is granted by a state to practice a profession; certification typically comes from a professional organization; registration lists qualified individuals. Good Samaritan laws protect people who give reasonable emergency aid in good faith without compensation, though protection does not cover gross negligence.','Working outside scope can harm patients and lead to liability or loss of credentials, even if the professional meant well. Delegation must match the delegate’s training and legal scope. Continuing education maintains competence. Licensing boards can suspend or revoke a license for unprofessional conduct, substance misuse, fraud or criminal acts, after due process.',['An off-duty certified nursing assistant stops to help at a car crash and provides basic first aid. Explain whether Good Samaritan protection applies.','If the aid is given in good faith, without compensation, and is reasonable and within training, Good Samaritan laws generally protect the helper. Protection would not cover gross negligence or reckless actions.'],[['Scope of practice','Legally permitted duties for a credential.',['scope','permit','duties']],['Good Samaritan law','Protects good-faith, uncompensated emergency aid.',['samaritan','good faith','emergency']],['Licensure','State authorization to practice a profession.',['licens','state','authoriz']]],['Who grants a professional license to practice?',['A state licensing authority','The patient','A hospital cafeteria','A textbook publisher'],'Licensure is a state function.'],['Good Samaritan laws generally do NOT protect against:',['Gross negligence','Reasonable first aid given in good faith','Calling emergency services','Staying with the injured person'],'Protection does not extend to reckless or grossly negligent acts.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Medical Math',[{title:'ISMP: error-prone abbreviations and dose designations',url:'https://www.ismp.org/recommendations/error-prone-abbreviations-list'},OS('prealgebra-2e','Prealgebra 2e')],[
 ['Unit 1 · Mathematical essentials','Mathematical essentials are 5% of the Medical Math test plan, but they power every other domain. Fractions, decimals, percents, ratios and rounding rules must be automatic. All numbers in this course are invented practice values, never patient instructions.',[
  ['Fractions, decimals, percents and ratios','A fraction, decimal and percent can express the same quantity: 1/4 equals 0.25 equals 25%. To convert a fraction to a decimal, divide the numerator by the denominator. To convert a decimal to a percent, multiply by 100. A ratio compares two quantities, such as 1:1000, and a proportion states that two ratios are equal, which allows solving for an unknown by cross-multiplying.','Safe notation prevents tenfold errors. Always write a leading zero before a decimal point, as in 0.5 mg, so the decimal is not missed. Never write a trailing zero, as in 5.0 mg, because it can be misread as 50 mg. Round only at the end of a calculation, following the rounding rule given in the problem. Estimating the expected answer first helps catch errors: if half a tablet is expected, an answer of 5 tablets is clearly wrong.',['Solve the proportion 2 : 5 = x : 40, and explain how you would check the answer.','Cross-multiply: 5x = 80, so x = 16. Check by confirming 2/5 equals 16/40, both 0.4. Estimating that x should be a bit less than half of 40 also confirms the answer is reasonable.'],[['Proportion','Two equal ratios let you solve for an unknown.',['proportion','ratio','cross']],['Leading zero','Write 0.5, never .5, to make decimals visible.',['leading','zero','decimal']],['Estimation','Predicting the answer catches calculation errors.',['estimat','check','reasonab']]],['Which is the safest way to write one-half milligram?',['0.5 mg','.5 mg','0.50 mg','1/2.0 mg'],'Use a leading zero and no trailing zero.'],['What is 3/8 expressed as a percent?',['37.5%','38%','0.375%','3.8%'],'3 divided by 8 is 0.375, or 37.5%.']],
  ['Systems of measurement and temperature','The metric system uses base units of grams for mass, liters for volume and meters for length, with prefixes that change by powers of ten: kilo is 1,000, milli is 1/1,000, and micro is 1/1,000,000. Household measures include teaspoons, tablespoons and cups; 1 teaspoon is about 5 mL and 1 tablespoon about 15 mL. One kilogram equals about 2.2 pounds and one inch equals 2.54 centimeters. Temperature converts by °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32.','Conversions within the metric system move the decimal point: grams to milligrams multiplies by 1,000. Between systems, use equivalents as conversion factors and keep track of units. Military or 24-hour time avoids AM and PM confusion: 1:30 PM is 1330 and midnight is 0000. Check whether a conversion should make the number larger or smaller: converting kilograms to pounds should produce a larger number.',['Convert a body temperature of 38.5 °C to Fahrenheit and explain a quick reasonableness check.','°F = (38.5 × 9/5) + 32 = 69.3 + 32 = 101.3 °F. Normal body temperature is about 37 °C or 98.6 °F, so a value a little higher in both scales is reasonable.'],[['Metric prefixes','Kilo, milli and micro change values by powers of ten.',['kilo','milli','micro']],['Conversion factors','Equivalents such as 2.2 lb per kg convert between systems.',['factor','equivalen','2.2']],['Temperature formulas','Use (°F − 32) × 5/9 and (°C × 9/5) + 32.',['celsius','fahrenheit','formula']]],['How many milligrams are in 0.25 g?',['250 mg','25 mg','2.5 mg','2,500 mg'],'Multiply grams by 1,000 to get milligrams.'],['A patient weighs 154 lb. What is the weight in kilograms?',['70 kg','339 kg','15.4 kg','7 kg'],'154 divided by 2.2 equals 70 kg.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Measurement and conversion problems','Measurement and conversion problems are 30% of the test plan. Dimensional analysis is the most reliable method: arrange conversion factors so unwanted units cancel and only the desired unit remains.',[
  ['Dimensional analysis','Dimensional analysis multiplies the starting quantity by conversion factors written as fractions equal to one, such as 1 kg / 2.2 lb. Each factor is placed so the unit to remove appears on the opposite side and cancels. Multiple factors can be chained in one setup, which reduces rounding steps and makes the reasoning visible.','Write units on every number. If the units do not cancel to the desired unit, the setup is wrong even if the arithmetic is right. This method works for weights, volumes, rates and dosage. For example, converting 3 tablespoons to milliliters uses 15 mL per tablespoon: 3 tbsp × 15 mL/1 tbsp = 45 mL. Checking the final unit is as important as checking the final number.',['Convert 2.5 cups to milliliters using 1 cup = 240 mL, showing the units cancel.','2.5 cups × 240 mL / 1 cup = 600 mL. The cup unit appears in the numerator and denominator and cancels, leaving milliliters as intended.'],[['Conversion factor','A fraction equal to one that changes units.',['factor','fraction','equal']],['Unit cancellation','Units on opposite sides of the fraction cancel.',['cancel','unit','opposite']],['Chaining','Several factors can be combined in one setup.',['chain','multiple','combin']]],['Using 1 inch = 2.54 cm, how many centimeters are in 10 inches?',['25.4 cm','3.94 cm','254 cm','12.54 cm'],'10 × 2.54 = 25.4 cm.'],['In dimensional analysis, a correct setup is confirmed when:',['Unwanted units cancel and the desired unit remains','The answer is a whole number','All units are in the numerator','No numbers are written'],'Units are the check on the setup.']],
  ['Intake, output and body measurements','Intake and output records track fluids in and out to assess fluid balance. Intake includes oral fluids, IV fluids and tube feedings; output includes urine, emesis and drainage. Foods that are liquid at room temperature, such as ice pops and gelatin, are usually counted as intake. Body mass index is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. Body surface area calculations also use height and weight.','Convert all values to milliliters before totaling. Ice chips are often counted as about half their volume as water, according to facility policy. A large positive balance, much more intake than output, may suggest fluid retention, while a negative balance may suggest dehydration; interpretation belongs to the clinical team. For BMI, square the height in meters first, then divide.',['A practice record shows 2 cups of juice (240 mL each), 250 mL of soup and 1,000 mL IV fluid; output is 1,450 mL urine. Calculate intake, output and balance.','Intake is 480 + 250 + 1,000 = 1,730 mL. Output is 1,450 mL. The balance is +280 mL, meaning intake exceeded output in this practice example.'],[['Intake','All fluids entering the body converted to mL.',['intake','oral','fluid']],['Output','Urine, emesis and drainage leaving the body.',['output','urine','drain']],['Fluid balance','Intake minus output shows net balance.',['balance','minus','net']]],['A person 1.6 m tall weighs 64 kg. What is the BMI?',['25','40','16','102'],'BMI = 64 ÷ (1.6 × 1.6) = 64 ÷ 2.56 = 25.'],['Which item is usually counted as fluid intake?',['Gelatin','Toast','Mashed potatoes','Rice'],'Foods liquid at room temperature count as intake.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Drug dosages and IV solutions','Drug dosages and intravenous solutions are the largest domain at 35% of the test plan. These calculations use invented values for arithmetic practice only; real medication decisions require an authorized order and professional verification.',[
  ['Oral and injectable dose calculations','The basic formula is desired dose ÷ dose on hand × quantity = amount to give, often written D/H × Q. The desired and on-hand doses must be in the same unit before dividing. For weight-based dosing, multiply the dose per kilogram by the weight in kilograms, then compare the result with the safe range. Reconstituted medications list a concentration after mixing, such as 250 mg per 5 mL.','Before calculating, estimate: if the desired dose is less than the dose on hand, the answer should be less than one tablet or less than the stated volume. Convert pounds to kilograms before weight-based calculations. Round according to the problem’s instructions and the measuring device; liquid doses are often rounded to tenths of a milliliter. Questioning an unusual result is part of safe practice, not a sign of weakness.',['Practice: an order is 375 mg, and the liquid on hand is 250 mg per 5 mL. Calculate the volume and check it.','375 ÷ 250 × 5 mL = 7.5 mL. The desired dose is 1.5 times the dose in 5 mL, so 1.5 × 5 = 7.5 mL confirms the answer.'],[['D/H × Q','Desired over have, times quantity, gives the amount.',['desired','have','quantity']],['Matching units','Convert to the same unit before dividing.',['unit','convert','same']],['Weight-based dosing','Dose per kg times weight in kg.',['weight','kg','per']]],['Practice: desired 0.5 g; tablets on hand are 250 mg each. How many tablets?',['2 tablets','0.5 tablet','20 tablets','5 tablets'],'0.5 g = 500 mg; 500 ÷ 250 = 2.'],['Practice: a dose is 10 mg/kg for a 30 kg child. What is the dose?',['300 mg','3 mg','40 mg','33 mg'],'10 × 30 = 300 mg.']],
  ['IV flow rates and infusion time','IV flow rate in mL per hour equals total volume ÷ time in hours. For gravity infusions, drops per minute = volume in mL × drop factor in gtt/mL ÷ time in minutes. Macrodrip sets commonly deliver 10, 15 or 20 drops per mL, and microdrip sets deliver 60 drops per mL. Infusion time equals total volume ÷ rate.','Convert hours to minutes for drop-rate calculations. Drops cannot be divided, so drip rates are rounded to whole numbers. With a microdrip set of 60 gtt/mL, drops per minute equal milliliters per hour, which is a useful check. Keep track of whether the question asks for mL/h, gtt/min, or total time, and label the answer with its unit.',['Practice: 1,000 mL is to infuse over 8 hours with a 15 gtt/mL set. Find mL/h and gtt/min.','Rate is 1,000 ÷ 8 = 125 mL/h. Drops per minute = 1,000 × 15 ÷ 480 = 31.25, rounded to 31 gtt/min.'],[['mL per hour','Total volume divided by hours.',['ml/h','hour','volume']],['Drop factor','Number of drops per mL for the tubing.',['drop','factor','gtt']],['Microdrip check','At 60 gtt/mL, gtt/min equals mL/h.',['microdrip','60','equal']]],['Practice: 500 mL over 4 hours equals what rate?',['125 mL/h','2,000 mL/h','12.5 mL/h','250 mL/h'],'500 ÷ 4 = 125 mL/h.'],['Practice: 60 mL/h with a 60 gtt/mL microdrip set equals:',['60 gtt/min','1 gtt/min','3,600 gtt/min','10 gtt/min'],'With a 60 gtt/mL set, drops per minute equal mL per hour.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Dilutions, solutions and concentrations','Dilutions, solutions and concentrations make up 30% of the test plan. Percent strength, ratio strength and the dilution formula C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ appear in both pharmacy and laboratory problems.',[
  ['Percent and ratio strength','A weight-in-volume percent solution gives grams per 100 mL: a 5% solution contains 5 g of solute per 100 mL. A ratio strength such as 1:1000 means 1 g in 1,000 mL. To find the grams needed, multiply the percent as a decimal by the total volume: 500 mL of a 5% solution needs 0.05 × 500 = 25 g.','Identify the type of percent before calculating: weight-in-volume (g/100 mL), volume-in-volume (mL/100 mL), or weight-in-weight (g/100 g). Converting between ratio and percent is straightforward: 1:1000 equals 0.1 g per 100 mL, or 0.1%. Normal saline is 0.9% sodium chloride, meaning 0.9 g per 100 mL, a useful benchmark for reasonableness.',['How many grams of sodium chloride are in 1,000 mL of 0.9% saline? Explain the setup.','0.9% means 0.9 g per 100 mL. For 1,000 mL: 0.9 g × 10 = 9 g. Alternatively 0.009 × 1,000 mL = 9 g.'],[['Weight-in-volume percent','Grams of solute per 100 mL of solution.',['percent','gram','100']],['Ratio strength','Expresses 1 part solute in a stated total.',['ratio','1:1000','part']],['Benchmark','0.9% saline gives a reasonableness check.',['saline','0.9','benchmark']]],['How many grams of solute are in 250 mL of a 2% solution?',['5 g','50 g','0.5 g','2 g'],'0.02 × 250 = 5 g.'],['A 1:100 ratio solution equals what percent?',['1%','10%','0.1%','100%'],'1 g per 100 mL equals 1%.']],
  ['Dilutions and C₁V₁ = C₂V₂','Diluting a stock solution lowers its concentration while the amount of solute stays the same. The formula C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ relates the stock concentration and volume to the final concentration and volume. To prepare a solution, solve for V₁, the volume of stock needed, then add diluent to reach the final volume. Serial dilutions repeat a fixed dilution, such as 1:10, so the overall dilution factor is the product of each step.','The final concentration must be lower than the stock; if a calculation suggests otherwise, the setup is reversed. The amount of diluent equals final volume minus stock volume. For serial dilutions, three successive 1:10 dilutions produce a 1:1000 total dilution. Units for concentration must match on both sides of the equation.',['Practice: prepare 200 mL of a 10% solution from a 50% stock. How much stock and diluent are needed?','V₁ = (10% × 200 mL) ÷ 50% = 40 mL stock. Add 160 mL of diluent to reach 200 mL. The final concentration is lower than the stock, as expected.'],[['C₁V₁ = C₂V₂','The amount of solute stays the same during dilution.',['c1v1','solute','same']],['Diluent volume','Final volume minus stock volume.',['diluent','minus','final']],['Serial dilution','Multiply each step’s factor for the total dilution.',['serial','factor','multiply']]],['Practice: how much 20% stock makes 100 mL of 5% solution?',['25 mL','400 mL','4 mL','75 mL'],'(5 × 100) ÷ 20 = 25 mL.'],['Two sequential 1:10 dilutions give a total dilution of:',['1:100','1:20','1:10','1:1000'],'10 × 10 = 100.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Medical Reading',[{title:'HOSA Medical Reading guidelines and current book list',url:'https://hosa.org/guidelines/'},{title:'CDC: Field Epidemiology Manual',url:'https://www.cdc.gov/field-epi-manual/'}],[
 ['Unit 1 · Reading strategy for the event','The Medical Reading test has 10 questions per selected book. These lessons teach a reading system that works for any title on the current HOSA list; they do not replace reading the books themselves.',[
  ['Planning to read five books','The event rewards detailed recall across several nonfiction books, so plan reading like training: divide each book into daily sections, schedule review days, and finish early enough to reread. Active reading means previewing the table of contents, reading with a question in mind, and pausing after each chapter to summarize. Spaced repetition, reviewing material at increasing intervals, strengthens long-term memory more than one long cram session.','Track what you read in a structured notebook: for each chapter record the main argument, key people, dates, places, numbers, scientific terms, and one memorable quote with its page. Questions often ask about specific details, cause and effect, and the author’s purpose. Self-testing, writing your own questions and answering them later from memory, is one of the most effective study methods because retrieval itself strengthens memory.',['You have eight weeks and five books. Design a reading and review plan using spaced repetition.','Read about one book every week for five weeks, writing chapter summaries and self-test questions. Use weeks six and seven to review each book’s notes at spaced intervals and quiz yourself, and use week eight for mixed review across all books and timed practice.'],[['Active reading','Reading with a purpose and summarizing each chapter.',['active','summar','purpose']],['Spaced repetition','Reviewing at increasing intervals improves memory.',['spaced','interval','review']],['Retrieval practice','Testing yourself strengthens recall.',['retriev','self-test','quiz']]],['Which study method most strengthens long-term memory?',['Spaced retrieval practice','One long cram session','Rereading only the first chapter','Highlighting every line'],'Spacing and self-testing outperform cramming.'],['A good chapter note for this event should include:',['Main argument, key people, numbers and terms','Only the chapter title','Only words you already know','A copy of the whole chapter'],'Structured notes capture testable details.']],
  ['Main ideas, details and author’s purpose','Every chapter has a main idea supported by details. The main idea answers what the author wants you to understand; supporting details provide evidence, examples and data. Nonfiction health books often combine narrative, the story of people and events, with exposition, explanation of science or policy. Author’s purpose may be to inform, persuade, or reflect, and many books do all three.','Distinguish facts, which can be verified, from opinions and interpretations. Watch for signal words: “however” signals contrast, “as a result” signals cause and effect, and “for example” introduces evidence. When a test question asks why something happened, look for the causal chain the author built across pages, not just one sentence. Re-reading the first and last paragraphs of a chapter often reveals its main idea.',['Practice passage: “Clinics began offering evening hours. As a result, missed appointments fell by one third, although staffing costs rose.” Identify the main idea, a supporting detail, and a cause-effect relationship.','The main idea is that evening hours reduced missed appointments. The detail is the one-third decrease. Offering evening hours caused fewer missed appointments, while the rise in staffing costs is a contrasting trade-off signaled by “although.”'],[['Main idea','What the author most wants the reader to understand.',['main','idea','central']],['Supporting details','Evidence, data and examples that support the main idea.',['detail','evidence','example']],['Signal words','Words like however and as a result reveal structure.',['signal','however','result']]],['The phrase “as a result” usually signals:',['Cause and effect','Contrast','Definition','Chronological list only'],'It introduces an outcome of a previous cause.'],['Which is a verifiable fact?',['The clinic opened in 2019','The clinic is the best in the state','Patients should feel grateful','The design is beautiful'],'A date can be checked; the others are opinions.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Reading about science and medicine','Health nonfiction explains science to general readers. Understanding how evidence is presented helps you remember details and answer interpretation questions.',[
  ['Reading epidemiology and outbreak narratives','Many health books describe outbreaks. Key ideas include the index case, transmission routes, incubation periods, contact tracing and interventions such as isolation and vaccination. Epidemiologists ask who is affected, where, and when, then form hypotheses about cause. The basic reproduction number, R₀, describes the average number of new infections caused by one case in a fully susceptible population.','When reading, build a timeline: when cases were detected, what investigators suspected, which evidence changed their minds, and which interventions worked. Note how uncertainty was handled, because authors often highlight decisions made with incomplete information. Distinguish the biology of a pathogen from the social factors, such as housing, travel and trust, that shape its spread.',['An outbreak narrative says cases fell after contact tracing and quarantine began. What additional evidence would strengthen the claim that these interventions caused the decline?','Comparison with a similar area without the interventions, timing that matches incubation periods, and data showing traced contacts became cases less often would strengthen a causal claim. Other changes, like seasons or behavior, must be considered.'],[['Contact tracing','Identifying people exposed to a case.',['contact','trac','expos']],['Incubation period','Time from exposure to symptoms.',['incubat','exposure','symptom']],['Timeline','Ordering events reveals cause and response.',['timeline','order','sequence']]],['What does R₀ describe?',['Average new infections from one case in a susceptible population','The number of hospitals','The death rate only','The incubation period'],'R₀ is the basic reproduction number.'],['Time between exposure and the first symptoms is the:',['Incubation period','Prodrome only','Recovery period','Quarantine'],'The incubation period precedes symptoms.']],
  ['Medical vocabulary in context','Nonfiction health books introduce technical vocabulary, often defined once and used throughout. Context clues help: a definition clue restates the term, an example clue illustrates it, and a contrast clue shows what it is not. Word parts also help: prefixes, roots and suffixes such as hepat-, -itis and -ectomy reveal meaning in unfamiliar terms.','Keep a running glossary for each book with the page number where each term is defined. Test questions may ask for the meaning of a term as the author used it, which can be narrower than a dictionary definition. When an author uses an acronym, record its full form at first use. Connecting new terms to the people and events in the story makes them easier to remember.',['Practice sentence: “Unlike acute infections, which resolve in days, chronic hepatitis can persist for years.” What does chronic mean here, and which clue reveals it?','Chronic means long-lasting or persisting over a long time. The contrast clue “unlike acute infections, which resolve in days” shows chronic is the opposite of short-term.'],[['Context clues','Definition, example and contrast clues reveal meaning.',['context','clue','contrast']],['Word parts','Prefixes, roots and suffixes break down terms.',['prefix','root','suffix']],['Glossary','Recording terms with page numbers aids review.',['glossary','page','record']]],['In “hepatitis,” the suffix -itis means:',['Inflammation','Removal','Study of','Enlargement'],'-itis indicates inflammation.'],['A contrast clue shows a word’s meaning by:',['Showing what it is not','Giving its spelling','Listing its rhymes','Translating it to Latin'],'Contrast clues reveal meaning through opposition.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · People, ethics and history in health stories','Health books often center on people: patients, clinicians, researchers and communities. Questions may ask about their decisions, motivations and the ethical issues their stories raise.',[
  ['Tracking people, places and decisions','Make a character map for each book: list each person, their role, where they worked, what they believed, and what decisions they made. Note relationships and conflicts, because authors use them to show how ideas spread or were resisted. Record dates and locations, since questions often ask when or where something happened.','Motivation explains decisions. A researcher might pursue a risky idea because of personal experience, professional rivalry or concern for patients. Ask what a person knew at the time, not what we know now; judging past decisions fairly requires historical context. When an author presents multiple perspectives, note how each one is supported with evidence.',['A book describes a physician who resisted a new hygiene practice that later proved effective. How should a reader analyze the physician’s decision?','Consider what evidence and beliefs were available at the time, the physician’s training and incentives, and how the new practice was presented. The analysis should explain the resistance in context while recognizing the harm it caused.'],[['Character map','A record of people, roles and decisions.',['character','map','role']],['Motivation','The reasons behind a person’s choices.',['motivat','reason','why']],['Historical context','What was known and believed at the time.',['context','histor','time']]],['Why is historical context important when judging past medical decisions?',['People acted on what was known at the time','Past decisions were always correct','Context never matters','It replaces evidence'],'Fair analysis considers knowledge available then.'],['A character map should record a person’s:',['Role, beliefs and key decisions','Shoe size','Favorite color only','Phone number'],'These details support comprehension questions.']],
  ['Ethics and equity themes in health nonfiction','Many health books explore research ethics, consent, inequity and trust. Historical abuses in research led to protections such as informed consent, institutional review boards and the Belmont Report’s principles of respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Stories of unequal access reveal how race, income, geography and stigma shape health outcomes.','Identify the theme an author develops across chapters, then find the scenes and data that support it. Distinguish the author’s argument from the events described. A strong essay answer, such as a tiebreaker, states a clear claim, supports it with specific details from the book, and explains why those details matter.',['Write a strong claim and support outline for an essay on how mistrust affected a community’s response to a health program described in a book.','Claim: past mistreatment created mistrust that reduced participation. Support: specific earlier events in the book, community members’ stated concerns, and participation data. Explanation: these details show trust must be rebuilt through transparency and community partnership.'],[['Theme','A central message developed across the book.',['theme','message','develop']],['Belmont principles','Respect for persons, beneficence and justice in research.',['belmont','respect','justice']],['Evidence-based claim','A clear claim supported by specific details.',['claim','support','specific']]],['Which document set out respect for persons, beneficence and justice for research?',['The Belmont Report','The Bill of Rights','The Hippocratic Oath only','A hospital menu'],'The Belmont Report established these research principles.'],['A strong essay answer should include:',['A clear claim supported by specific details','Only the book’s title','Unrelated opinions','A list of chapter numbers'],'Specific evidence supports the claim.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Test performance','The written test is timed. Practical test strategy turns your reading into points.',[
  ['Answering detail and inference questions','Detail questions ask for something stated directly, such as a date or name. Inference questions ask what can be concluded from the text even though it is not stated outright. For both, read every option before choosing, eliminate clearly wrong answers, and watch for options that are true in general but not supported by the book.','Extreme words such as “always” and “never” often signal a wrong option unless the author used them. If two options seem right, choose the more specific one supported by the text. Pace yourself with a time budget per question, mark difficult items, and return to them. Never leave a question blank unless the rules penalize guessing.',['A question asks why a researcher moved to a new lab. One option is true in real life but not mentioned in the book. How should you decide?','Choose the option supported by the book’s account, not outside knowledge. The test measures comprehension of the text, so an answer must be grounded in what the author wrote.'],[['Detail question','Answer is stated directly in the text.',['detail','stated','direct']],['Inference question','Answer is concluded from evidence in the text.',['inference','conclud','evidence']],['Elimination','Removing clearly wrong options improves accuracy.',['eliminat','wrong','remove']]],['Which answer choice is often a warning sign?',['One with absolute words like “always” not used by the author','The most specific supported option','One quoting a supported detail','One matching the timeline'],'Absolute words often make an option too broad.'],['An inference question asks for:',['A conclusion supported by evidence in the text','An exact quote only','The author’s birthday','A random guess'],'Inference goes beyond explicit statements but stays grounded in evidence.']],
  ['Review systems and tiebreaker essays','In the final weeks, consolidate notes into one-page summaries per book with the timeline, character map, key terms and major themes. Interleaved practice, mixing questions from different books, improves the ability to retrieve the right information. Teaching a chapter to someone else reveals gaps in understanding.','For an essay tiebreaker, plan for two minutes: a thesis, two or three specific supporting details, and a conclusion that answers the prompt. Specific names, numbers and events demonstrate deep reading. Organize clearly with paragraphs and transitions, and reread to correct errors if time allows.',['Outline a five-minute essay plan for the prompt: “Which book on your list best shows how one person changed public health?”','Thesis naming the book and person. Body paragraph one: the problem before their work, with specific details. Body paragraph two: their actions and evidence of impact. Conclusion: why this example best answers the prompt.'],[['One-page summary','Condensed timeline, people, terms and themes.',['summary','condens','one-page']],['Interleaving','Mixing practice across books improves retrieval.',['interleav','mix','retriev']],['Essay structure','Thesis, specific support and conclusion.',['thesis','support','conclusion']]],['Interleaved practice means:',['Mixing questions from different books','Studying one book only','Reading without notes','Sleeping during review'],'Interleaving mixes topics to improve flexible recall.'],['What makes a tiebreaker essay stronger?',['Specific details that support a clear thesis','Very long sentences','Repeating the prompt','Avoiding any examples'],'Specific evidence shows deep reading.']]
 ]]
]);

course('Medical Spelling',[{title:'MedlinePlus: understanding medical words',url:'https://medlineplus.gov/medicalwords.html'},OS('anatomy-and-physiology-2e','Anatomy and Physiology 2e')],[
 ['Unit 1 · How medical words are built','Medical Spelling has a written test followed by a live spelldown. Spelling becomes far easier when you understand word structure: most medical terms combine a root with prefixes, suffixes and combining vowels.',[
  ['Roots, combining forms and combining vowels','A word root carries the core meaning, such as cardi for heart or gastr for stomach. A combining vowel, usually o, joins roots to suffixes or to other roots, creating a combining form such as cardi/o. Use the combining vowel before a suffix that begins with a consonant, as in cardiology, but usually drop it before a suffix beginning with a vowel, as in carditis. Between two roots the combining vowel is usually kept, as in gastroenterology.','These rules explain many spellings that look irregular. Gastr + -ectomy becomes gastrectomy because the suffix starts with a vowel. Oste + arthr + -itis becomes osteoarthritis, keeping the o between roots. Learning a root once lets you spell dozens of related words, so study word families instead of isolated lists. Saying each word part aloud as you write it builds the link between sound and spelling needed for the spelldown.',['Build and spell the term meaning “inflammation of the stomach and intestines” from gastr/o, enter/o and -itis, explaining each combining-vowel choice.','Gastroenteritis: gastr + o + enter + itis. The o is kept between the two roots, and dropped before -itis because the suffix begins with a vowel.'],[['Word root','Carries the core meaning of the term.',['root','meaning','core']],['Combining vowel','Usually o; kept between roots and before consonant suffixes.',['vowel','combin','between']],['Suffix rule','Drop the combining vowel before a vowel-initial suffix.',['suffix','drop','vowel']]],['Which spelling correctly combines arthr/o and -itis?',['Arthritis','Arthroitis','Arthitis','Artheritis'],'The combining vowel is dropped before -itis.'],['Which spelling correctly combines cardi/o and -logy?',['Cardiology','Cardilogy','Cardeology','Kardiology'],'Keep the o before a consonant-initial suffix.']],
  ['Prefixes and suffixes that change meaning','Prefixes come before the root and often indicate number, position, time or direction: hypo- means below or deficient, hyper- means above or excessive, peri- means around and endo- means within. Suffixes come at the end and often indicate a condition or procedure: -ectomy is surgical removal, -otomy is cutting into, -ostomy is creating an opening, -algia is pain, and -emia is a blood condition.','Similar-sounding parts cause many spelling errors. Hypo- and hyper- differ by one letter but have opposite meanings. The procedure suffixes -ectomy, -otomy and -ostomy describe removal, incision and creating an opening, so spelling them correctly also preserves meaning. Some suffixes come in pairs: -graphy for the recording process and -gram for the record itself.',['Explain the difference in spelling and meaning among colectomy, colotomy and colostomy.','Colectomy is removal of part or all of the colon. Colotomy is an incision into the colon. Colostomy is creating an opening from the colon to the body surface. The suffix spelling directly changes the procedure described.'],[['Hypo- vs hyper-','Below versus above; opposite meanings.',['hypo','hyper','opposite']],['Procedure suffixes','-ectomy, -otomy and -ostomy describe different procedures.',['ectomy','otomy','ostomy']],['-graphy vs -gram','The process of recording versus the record.',['graphy','gram','record']]],['Which suffix means surgical removal?',['-ectomy','-otomy','-ostomy','-plasty'],'-ectomy means excision or removal.'],['Which prefix means excessive or above normal?',['Hyper-','Hypo-','Peri-','Endo-'],'Hyper- indicates above normal.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 2 · Spelling by body system','Organizing terms by body system connects spelling to meaning and helps with both the written test and the spelldown.',[
  ['Cardiovascular, respiratory and blood terms','Common roots include angi/o and vas/o for vessel, arteri/o for artery, phleb/o and ven/o for vein, pneum/o and pneumon/o for lung or air, bronch/o for bronchus, and hem/o and hemat/o for blood. Frequently misspelled terms include sphygmomanometer, auscultation, arrhythmia, tachycardia, pneumothorax, hemorrhage and thrombocytopenia.','Notice silent and doubled letters: pneumonia begins with a silent p, arrhythmia has a double r and the letters rh, and hemorrhage has a double r followed by h. Sphygmomanometer combines sphygm/o, pulse, with manometer, pressure-measuring device. Thrombocytopenia breaks into thromb/o (clot), cyt/o (cell) and -penia (deficiency), which makes its long spelling manageable.',['Break thrombocytopenia into parts and explain how the parts help you spell and define it.','Thromb/o means clot, cyt/o means cell, and -penia means deficiency: a deficiency of clotting cells, or platelets. Spelling each part in order, thrombo-cyto-penia, reproduces the full word.'],[['Silent letters','Pneumonia and pneumothorax begin with a silent p.',['silent','pneum','p']],['Doubled letters','Arrhythmia and hemorrhage contain rr followed by h.',['double','rr','rrh']],['Word breakdown','Long terms become manageable when split into parts.',['part','split','break']]],['Which is spelled correctly?',['Hemorrhage','Hemorage','Hemmorhage','Hemorrage'],'Hemorrhage has rrh.'],['Which is spelled correctly?',['Arrhythmia','Arythmia','Arrythmia','Arhythmia'],'Arrhythmia has rrh followed by y.']],
  ['Digestive, musculoskeletal and nervous system terms','Digestive roots include gastr/o, enter/o, col/o, hepat/o, cholecyst/o and esophag/o. Musculoskeletal roots include oste/o for bone, my/o for muscle, arthr/o for joint, chondr/o for cartilage and ten/o and tendin/o for tendon. Nervous system roots include neur/o, encephal/o, cerebr/o and mening/o. Challenging terms include cholecystectomy, esophagogastroduodenoscopy, osteomyelitis, encephalopathy and meningitis.','Some roots have two spellings in use: tendon uses ten/o and tendin/o, so tendinitis is the preferred spelling while tendonitis is also seen. Esophagogastroduodenoscopy strings four roots and a suffix; spell it by parts: esophago-gastro-duodeno-scopy. Plurals follow Greek and Latin rules: one vertebra, two vertebrae; one bronchus, two bronchi; one diagnosis, two diagnoses.',['Spell the plural forms of vertebra, bronchus and diagnosis, and state the rule for each.','Vertebrae: Latin -a becomes -ae. Bronchi: -us becomes -i. Diagnoses: Greek -is becomes -es.'],[['Latin plurals','-a becomes -ae; -us becomes -i.',['plural','ae','latin']],['Greek plurals','-is becomes -es.',['greek','is','es']],['Multi-root terms','Spell long terms root by root.',['root','long','part']]],['What is the plural of diagnosis?',['Diagnoses','Diagnosises','Diagnosi','Diagnosae'],'Words ending in -is change to -es.'],['Which spelling is correct?',['Cholecystectomy','Cholecystecomy','Colecystectomy','Cholycystectomy'],'Chole (bile) + cyst (bladder) + ectomy (removal).']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 3 · Commonly confused and tricky terms','Many medical spelling errors involve words that sound alike but are spelled differently and mean different things. Precision protects meaning in patient records.',[
  ['Homonyms and sound-alikes','Some terms sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning. Ileum is part of the small intestine, while ilium is part of the hip bone. Perineal refers to the perineum, while peroneal refers to the fibula region. Mucus is the noun for the secretion, while mucous is the adjective, as in mucous membrane. Dysphagia means difficulty swallowing, while dysphasia means difficulty with speech.','Link each spelling to a memory cue: ileum with an e for “eating” connects it to digestion, and ilium with an i for “hip” connects it to bone. In the spelldown, definitions or sentences are often provided; listen carefully for context. If a word could be either sound-alike, ask for the definition or use in a sentence if the rules allow.',['A sentence states, “The specimen showed thick ____ from the airway.” Should it be mucus or mucous? Explain.','Mucus, because the sentence needs a noun naming the secretion. Mucous is an adjective, as in mucous membrane.'],[['Ileum vs ilium','Small intestine versus hip bone.',['ileum','ilium','intestine']],['Mucus vs mucous','Noun for secretion versus adjective.',['mucus','mucous','noun']],['Dysphagia vs dysphasia','Swallowing difficulty versus speech difficulty.',['dysphagia','dysphasia','swallow']]],['Which term refers to part of the small intestine?',['Ileum','Ilium','Illium','Ilieum'],'Ileum is the final portion of the small intestine.'],['Difficulty swallowing is spelled:',['Dysphagia','Dysphasia','Disphagia','Dysfagia'],'-phagia relates to eating or swallowing.']],
  ['Eponyms, abbreviations and pronunciation','Eponyms are terms named after people, such as Alzheimer disease or Parkinson disease; current style often drops the possessive. Abbreviations vary by facility, and some are on do-not-use lists because they cause errors. Pronunciation clues help spelling: ph is pronounced f, as in phlebitis; ps at the start sounds like s, as in psychology; and pt at the start sounds like t, as in pterygium.','For the spelldown, slow down and segment the word into syllables and word parts before speaking. Repeat the word after the pronouncer if permitted to confirm you heard it correctly. Know letters that are silent or unexpected: the initial p in pneumonia, the g in phlegm, and the ch pronounced k in cholesterol and chronic.',['Explain how pronunciation patterns help you spell psychiatry, phlebotomy and chronic correctly.','Psychiatry begins with silent p before s, phlebotomy uses ph for the f sound, and chronic uses ch for the k sound. Knowing these Greek-derived patterns prevents spelling by sound alone.'],[['Greek patterns','ph sounds like f; initial ps and pn have a silent p.',['ph','silent','greek']],['Eponyms','Terms named after people, often without possessives.',['eponym','named','person']],['Segmenting','Breaking words into syllables and parts before spelling.',['segment','syllable','part']]],['Which word begins with a silent letter?',['Psychology','Phlebitis','Chronic','Cardiac'],'The p in psychology is silent.'],['Which spelling is correct?',['Phlegm','Flegm','Phlem','Phleghm'],'Phlegm contains ph and a silent g.']]
 ]],
 ['Unit 4 · Competition practice','The written test and spelldown reward accuracy under pressure. Systematic practice builds both knowledge and composure.',[
  ['Building a study list and practice routine','Build a personal list from the official references, organized by body system and word part. Practice using look, say, cover, write and check: look at the word, say it in parts, cover it, write it from memory, then check. Track misspelled words in an error log and review them more often. Short daily sessions with self-testing outperform long, irregular sessions.','Analyze your errors by type: wrong combining vowel, missing doubled letter, silent letter, or confusion with a sound-alike. Each error type has a specific fix, such as reviewing combining vowel rules. Practice aloud and in writing because the event includes both formats. Study definitions too, since knowing meaning helps distinguish similar terms.',['Your error log shows you often misspell words with rrh and silent p. Design a targeted practice plan.','Group rrh words such as hemorrhage, arrhythmia and diarrhea, and silent-p words such as pneumonia and psychosis. Practice each group daily with look-say-cover-write-check, review the error log every other day, and test mixed lists weekly.'],[['Look-say-cover-write-check','A self-testing routine for spelling.',['look','cover','check']],['Error log','Tracking mistakes by type to target practice.',['error','log','track']],['Error types','Combining vowels, doubled letters and silent letters each need different fixes.',['type','vowel','silent']]],['Which practice routine involves writing a word from memory and checking it?',['Look, say, cover, write, check','Reading the list once','Copying while looking','Skipping difficult words'],'This routine uses active recall.'],['Why categorize spelling errors by type?',['Each type has a specific fix','It makes the list longer','It avoids practice','Spelling has no patterns'],'Targeted fixes are more efficient.']],
  ['Spelldown performance and composure','In a spelldown, competitors spell words aloud. Listen to the full pronunciation, ask for a repeat, definition or use in a sentence if the rules allow, and visualize the word in parts before beginning. Speak clearly and at a steady pace; once letters are spoken they usually cannot be changed, depending on the rules.','Composure supports accuracy. Slow breathing before your turn reduces anxiety. If you do not know a word, use structure: identify likely roots, prefixes and suffixes and apply combining vowel rules. Avoid rushing through familiar-sounding words, because sound-alikes are common traps. Review the event’s rules on whether you may restart a word.',['You hear a word you have never studied that sounds like “oss-tee-oh-kon-DROH-mah.” How can you reason through the spelling?','Identify oste/o for bone, chondr/o for cartilage and -oma for tumor. Combine them with combining vowels: osteochondroma. Visualize the parts, then spell steadily.'],[['Ask for context','Repeat, definition or sentence clarifies the word.',['context','definition','repeat']],['Visualize parts','Picture roots and suffixes before speaking.',['visual','part','root']],['Composure','Steady breathing and pacing support accuracy.',['composure','calm','breath']]],['Before spelling an unfamiliar word aloud, you should:',['Break it into likely word parts','Spell as fast as possible','Guess without thinking','Spell only the first syllable'],'Word structure guides spelling of unfamiliar terms.'],['Which suffix means tumor?',['-oma','-itis','-algia','-ectomy'],'-oma indicates a tumor or mass.']]
 ]]
]);
